Lab 11: Networking with Neutron (Basic)
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Configure tenant networks and subnets in OpenStack using Neutron.
Set up routers for external connectivity.
Assign floating IPs to instances for external access.
Prerequisites
An active OpenStack cloud environment with admin/tenant privileges.
OpenStack CLI tools installed (python-openstackclient).
Basic familiarity with Linux command line and OpenStack concepts.
Task 1: Create Tenant Networks and Subnets
Subtask 1.1: Create a Private Network
Command:

openstack network create --internal <NETWORK_NAME>
Example:

openstack network create --internal lab_network
--internal: Marks the network as private (non-external).
Expected Outcome:
A new private network is created. Note the ID and Name from the output.

Troubleshooting:

If you lack permissions, ensure you’re using the correct project/tenant credentials.
Verify Neutron services are running (openstack network agent list).
Subtask 1.2: Create a Subnet
Command:

openstack subnet create --network <NETWORK_NAME> \
--subnet-range <CIDR> \
--dns-nameserver <DNS_IP> <SUBNET_NAME>
Example:

openstack subnet create --network lab_network \
--subnet-range 192.168.1.0/24 \
--dns-nameserver 8.8.8.8 lab_subnet
Key Concepts:

--subnet-range: CIDR notation for the subnet (e.g., 192.168.1.0/24).
--dns-nameserver: DNS resolver (e.g., Google’s 8.8.8.8).
Expected Outcome:
A subnet is attached to the network. Verify with:

openstack subnet list
Task 2: Configure Routers for External Connectivity
Subtask 2.1: Create a Router
Command:

openstack router create <ROUTER_NAME>
Example:

openstack router create lab_router
Expected Outcome:
A new router is created. Note its ID.

Subtask 2.2: Attach Router to External Network
Identify the External Network:

openstack network list --external
Look for a network with router:external=True.
Attach Router:

openstack router set --external-gateway <EXTERNAL_NETWORK> <ROUTER_NAME>
Example:

openstack router set --external-gateway public lab_router
Expected Outcome:
The router now has external connectivity. Verify with:

openstack router show lab_router
Subtask 2.3: Connect Router to Private Subnet
Command:

openstack router add subnet <ROUTER_NAME> <SUBNET_NAME>
Example:

openstack router add subnet lab_router lab_subnet
Expected Outcome:
The private subnet is now routed externally. Check interfaces:

openstack port list --router lab_router
Task 3: Set Up Floating IPs for External Access
Subtask 3.1: Allocate a Floating IP
Command:

openstack floating ip create <EXTERNAL_NETWORK>
Example:

openstack floating ip create public
Expected Outcome:
A floating IP is allocated. Note the IP address from the output.

Subtask 3.2: Assign Floating IP to an Instance
List Instances:

openstack server list
Assign IP:

openstack server add floating ip <INSTANCE_NAME> <FLOATING_IP>
Example:

openstack server add floating ip vm1 203.0.113.10
Verify Connectivity:

ping <FLOATING_IP>
Ensure security groups allow ICMP/SSH.
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Created a tenant network and subnet using Neutron.
Configured a router to bridge private and external networks.
Assigned floating IPs to enable external access.
Key Takeaways:

Neutron abstracts network management in OpenStack.
Routers enable inter-subnet and external traffic.
Floating IPs provide public accessibility to instances.
Next Steps
Advanced Neutron features (LBaaS, VPNaaS).
Explore security groups and network policies.

